==========================================
SYSADMIN DAY 01 — TMUX (TERMINAL MULTIPLEXER)
Commands Reference Sheet
==========================================

--- STARTING / LEAVING ---
tmux                        # start a new, unnamed session
tmux new -s <name>           # start a new, NAMED session (always name it)
Ctrl+b  d                     # detach (leaves it running in the background)
tmux attach -t <name>          # reattach to a named session
tmux a -t <name>                # same, shorthand

--- MANAGING SESSIONS ---
tmux ls                       # list all sessions (name, windows, size, attached?)
tmux kill-session -t <name>    # kill one named session
tmux kill-server                # kill tmux entirely, all sessions gone

--- WINDOWS (tabs within one session) ---
Ctrl+b  c       create a new window
Ctrl+b  ,       rename the current window
Ctrl+b  n       next window
Ctrl+b  p       previous window
Ctrl+b  0-9     jump to window number 0-9
Ctrl+b  w       interactive list of all windows
Ctrl+b  &       close the current window (asks to confirm)

--- PANES (splits within one window) ---
Ctrl+b  %        split vertically (side by side)
Ctrl+b  "        split horizontally (stacked)
Ctrl+b  <arrow>   move to the pane in that direction
Ctrl+b  o          cycle to the next pane
Ctrl+b  z           zoom current pane fullscreen (press again to unzoom)
Ctrl+b  x            close current pane (asks to confirm)
Ctrl+b  q             briefly show pane numbers

--- WHY IT MATTERS ---
Everything running inside a tmux session keeps running even if:
  - you close the terminal app
  - your SSH connection drops
  - you log out and back in (as long as the machine itself stays up)
This is why tmux matters most on a REMOTE server reached over SSH (Networking Day 18-19) -
a long-running command (a download, a scan, a backup) survives a dropped connection.

--- QUICK REFERENCE: THE PREFIX KEY ---
Every tmux shortcut starts with the PREFIX, Ctrl+b by default: press Ctrl+b,
release both keys, THEN press the next key (e.g. d, c, %). It's not held together.

